        "content": "```json\n{\n  \"status\": \"partial\",\n  \"material_usefulness\": \"supporting\",\n  \"useful_material\": \"This review explicitly identifies the absence of standardized protocols for metabolomic analysis as a significant technical challenge in microbiome-metabolite research (Section 10.7). The authors note that variability in sample collection, storage conditions, metabolite extraction procedures, analytical platforms, and data normalization strategies contributes to inter-study inconsistency and impairs reproducibility. Section 6.2 describes analytical platforms used for metabolite profiling including LC-MS, GC-MS, and NMR spectroscopy for detecting SCFAs and other microbial metabolites in stool, plasma, serum, and urine. The paper states that 'the establishment of standardized sampling protocols, time-controlled study designs, and robust normalization strategies is essential to improve the clinical applicability of microbiome-derived metabolite biomarkers.' Table 2 summarizes advantages and limitations of various metabolomic techniques. Section 8.3 discusses methodological and translational barriers in biomarker standardization, noting that successful integration requires rigorous assessment of analytical robustness, scalability, and harmonized reporting standards.\",\n  \"auxiliary_material\": \"The review provides context on cancer-specific metabolite signatures across multiple malignancies (Table 3), including SCFA alterations in colorectal cancer (butyrate levels reported as low in CRC), indole derivatives in lung cancer, and polyamines in prostate cancer. Section 9 discusses therapeutic modulation strategies including postbiotics (direct delivery of purified microbial metabolites like butyrate, propionate, indole-3 derivatives). The paper mentions that targeted metabolomics platforms are increasingly employed for absolute quantification of predefined microbial metabolites including bile acids, SCFAs, polyamines, and tryptophan derivatives, but successful implementation requires careful consideration of biological variability, temporal dynamics, and standardization constraints. The review notes that much current evidence is cross-sectional and association-based, limiting causal inference.\",\n  \"remaining_gap\": \"This material does not provide specific standardized measurement protocols, reference ranges, or consensus guidelines for absolute concentration measurement of inosine or SCFAs in human tumor microenvironment or serum. The paper explicitly confirms this gap remains unresolved, stating that standardized protocols for metabolomic analysis are absent and that large-scale, multicenter validation and harmonized reporting standards are essential before widespread clinical adoption can be achieved. No specific consensus statements from regulatory bodies or professional organizations are cited. The review indicates this is an active research priority rather than an established standard.\",\n  \"outline_action\": \"This material supports documenting that standardization challenges exist in microbiome-metabolite research and provides information on available analytical platforms (LC-MS, GC-MS, NMR) and their respective advantages/limitations for metabolite quantification. It can be used to explain why consensus guidelines have not yet emerged and what methodological harmonization would require. The review's explicit acknowledgment of standardization gaps can support a 'partial' status conclusion. However, additional sources would be needed to identify any emerging consensus efforts, specific protocol recommendations from professional organizations, or validated reference ranges for specific metabolites in clinical samples.\"\n}\n```",
